Half the SEO tutorials for Next.js are still teaching you to import next/head and drop <title> tags into it. That code doesn't belong in an App Router project. There's no Head component in app/, and pasting one in gets you nothing — the tags just render into the body and Google sees a page with no title.
I hit this migrating a small SaaS blog from the Pages Router. Everything “worked” locally, but the OG previews were blank and the canonical tags were missing. The fix was to stop thinking about <head> as something you render, and start treating metadata as data you return.
Here’s how SEO actually works in Next.js 16 (App Router), with code I built and ran, not pseudocode.
Static metadata lives in the layout
The root layout owns the site-wide defaults. You export a metadata object and Next.js turns it into the right tags. The title.template bit is the part people miss — it wraps every child page's title so you don't repeat the brand name everywhere.
// app/layout.tsx
import type { Metadata, Viewport } from "next";export const metadata: Metadata = {
metadataBase: new URL("https://acme.dev"),
title: {
default: "Acme — analytics for indie SaaS",
template: "%s | Acme",
},
description:
"Acme gives solo founders revenue analytics without the enterprise pricing.",
openGraph: {
type: "website",
siteName: "Acme",
images: ["/og-default.png"],
},
twitter: { card: "summary_large_image" },
};export const viewport: Viewport = {
width: "device-width",
initialScale: 1,
};export default function RootLayout({
children,
}: Readonly<{ children: React.ReactNode }>) {
return (
<html lang="en">
<body>{children}</body>
</html>
);
}Two things that bit me here. metadataBase is not optional if you use relative image paths — leave it out and your og:image renders as a relative URL that no scraper can resolve. And viewport moved out of metadata into its own export a few versions back; if you still have it inside the metadata object you'll get a build warning telling you to move it.
Per-page metadata is a function, and params is now a Promise
For a blog post you don’t know the title until you fetch the post. That’s what generateMetadata is for. The thing that trips up people coming from older tutorials: in current Next.js, params is a Promise. You await it. If you destructure it synchronously the types won't even compile.
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateMetadata({
params,
}: {
params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
}): Promise<Metadata> {
const { slug } = await params;
const post = await getPost(slug); return {
title: post.title,
description: post.excerpt,
alternates: { canonical: `/blog/${post.slug}` },
openGraph: {
type: "article",
publishedTime: post.publishedAt,
images: [post.cover],
},
};
}The alternates.canonical line is the one to not skip. Every duplicate-content problem I've seen on a Next.js blog traces back to a missing canonical — the same post reachable at /blog/x and /blog/x?ref=twitter gets indexed twice and splits its own ranking.
When I built this and curled the rendered page, the <head> came back with exactly what I'd expect: <title>How I shipped in a weekend | Acme</title> (template applied), the canonical link, and the full set of og: and twitter: tags. The template composition happens server-side, so the crawler gets it in the initial HTML — no JS execution required.
JSON-LD: still just a script tag, but put it in the component
Structured data doesn’t go through the metadata API. You render a <script type="application/ld+json"> in the component itself. The one rule: use dangerouslySetInnerHTML with JSON.stringify, not a JSX child, or React will HTML-escape your JSON and Google's parser will choke on it.
export default async function BlogPost({
params,
}: {
params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
}) {
const { slug } = await params;
const post = await getPost(slug); const jsonLd = {
"@context": "https://schema.org",
"@type": "BlogPosting",
headline: post.title,
description: post.excerpt,
datePublished: post.publishedAt,
image: `https://acme.dev${post.cover}`,
}; return (
<article>
<script
type="application/ld+json"
dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: JSON.stringify(jsonLd) }}
/>
<h1>{post.title}</h1>
<Image src={post.cover} alt={post.title} width={1200} height={630} priority />
<p>{post.excerpt}</p>
</article>
);
}In the rendered HTML the block came through verbatim — unescaped, valid JSON, ready for Google’s Rich Results test. Note the absolute URL in image; schema.org wants a full URL, not a path.
Sitemap and robots are files now, not serverless functions
The old pattern was a getServerSideProps handler that hand-built XML. Gone. You drop an app/sitemap.ts that returns an array, and Next.js serves valid XML at /sitemap.xml. It can be async, so you fetch your slugs and map them.
// app/sitemap.ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from "next";const BASE = "https://acme.dev";export default async function sitemap(): Promise<MetadataRoute.Sitemap> {
const slugs = await getPostSlugs(); const posts: MetadataRoute.Sitemap = slugs.map((slug) => ({
url: `${BASE}/blog/${slug}`,
lastModified: new Date(),
changeFrequency: "weekly",
priority: 0.7,
})); return [
{ url: BASE, lastModified: new Date(), changeFrequency: "daily", priority: 1 },
...posts,
];
}// app/robots.ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from "next";export default function robots(): MetadataRoute.Robots {
return {
rules: { userAgent: "*", allow: "/", disallow: "/admin/" },
sitemap: "https://acme.dev/sitemap.xml",
};
}I hit /sitemap.xml on the running server and got a proper <urlset> document with the correct <loc>, <changefreq> and <priority> for each entry — no XML written by hand. /robots.txt came out equally clean with the Sitemap: line pointing back at it. Point robots.sitemap at the real URL so crawlers find the map without you submitting it manually.
The one that costs you Core Web Vitals
Speed is an actual ranking signal now, and the two easiest wins are already in the framework. Use next/image for anything above the fold and mark it priority so it isn't lazy-loaded — the largest image on the page is usually your Largest Contentful Paint, and lazy-loading it is self-sabotage. In the rendered output that priority flag turned into a <link rel="preload" as="image"> with a responsive imageSrcSet, exactly what you want the browser to see first.
For heavy client-only chunks — a comment widget, a chart — use next/dynamic so they don't sit in the initial bundle:
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";const CommentSection = dynamic(() => import("./comment-section"));What I’d tell my past self
If you’re copying an SEO snippet and it imports next/head, close the tab. That API doesn't exist in the App Router, and everything it did is now either a metadata export, a generateMetadata function, or a file convention. The whole model flipped from "render tags" to "return data," and once it clicks, the boilerplate mostly disappears — the framework generates the <head>, the sitemap, and the robots file for you.
The gotcha that’ll waste your afternoon: params is a Promise. Await it, or nothing typechecks.
